/ActualText (Technologies in AMRs:)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Navigation and Mapping: Simultaneous Localization and Mapping \(SLAM\) is a fundamental technology that allows AMRs to create a map of an unknown environment while simultaneously keeping track of their location within that environment. SLAM algorithms use data from various sensors to build and update maps in real-time. The Global Positioning System \(GPS\) primarily used for outdoor navigation can assist AMRs in determining their precise location in open environments, though it is less effective indoors due to signal interference. Inertial Measurement Units \(IMUs\) provide data on the robot's orientation, acceleration, and angular velocity, crucial for maintaining stability and aiding navigation algorithms.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Sensors: LiDAR \(Light Detection and Ranging\) sensors emit laser pulses to measure distances to surrounding objects, creating detailed 3D maps of the environment. LiDAR is essential for accurate navigation and obstacle detection. Vision systems using monocular, stereo, or depth cameras capture visual information from the environment, used for object recognition, navigation, and interaction with human operators. Ultrasonic and Infrared Sensors detect nearby objects by measuring the time it takes for emitted sound waves or infrared light to bounce back, often used for close-range obstacle detection and avoidance.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Human-Robot Interaction: A Graphical User Interface \(GUI\) is a visual interface that allows users to interact with a system through graphical elements such as buttons, icons, and menus. It provides an intuitive and user-friendly way to control and monitor devices, including AMRs, without requiring knowledge of complex commands or programming.)
/E (Human-Robot Interaction: A Graphical User Interface \(GUI\) is a visual interface that allows users to interact with a system through graphical elements such as buttons, icons, and menus. It provides an intuitive and user-friendly way to control and monitor devices, including AMRs, without requiring knowledge of complex commands or programming.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [0]
>>
